FXZS60 NSTU 070106

AFDPPG



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Pago Pago  AS

206 PM SST Wed Aug 6 2025



.Short term...

Thunderstorms scattered over the American Samoa region produced

heavy showers around noon, reaching nearly an inch of rain. This 

prompted a Flash Flood Warning through 4 pm. Gusty winds were also

observed at WSO Pago Pago, showing winds up to 25 mph during a

thunderstorm updraft. Although the atmosphere still appears to be

convective, thunderstorms are observed to be short-lived via 

satellite trends. Model data depicts showers diminishing tonight 

as a mid-level high pressure builds in the area. The flash flood 

watch will hold until thunderstorms have dissipated. The mid-

level high pressure will dominate over the territory through the 

new week. Winds will become light and variable Thursday as the 

trough continues to diminish in the area. 



.Long term...

By Friday, winds are forecast to remain from the southeast as a 

ridge builds near New Zealand. This feature will keep breezy 

trades and scattered showers through the new week. As the high 

pressure continues to move east, winds will then turn east on 

Sunday. 



&&

.Marine...

A strong south swell is still evident on PacIOOS buoy data showing

peak periods around 16 seconds. These long periods mean high surfs

are likely as well as strong rip currents. Expect swells to

gradually subside in the area Thursday, which will likely

diminish the high surf warning to an advisory. Seas will fall

below advisory thresholds by the weekend. However, breezy

conditions may keep a small craft advisory for mariners through

the new week. 



.PPG WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

High Surf Warning 

Flash Flood Watch 

High Surf Advisory 

Small Craft Advisory 



&&



