Denver/Boulder Aviation Discussion

Issued at 0021 UTC Thursday, August  7, 2025



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS through 00Z Friday/...

Issued at 540 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025

Early evening virga showers are clearing out across the plains,

with a few showers still present across the Palmer Divide. The

main threat for any additional microbursts passing over the TAF

sites is diminishing quickly, but outflow boundaries visible on

radar still leave a threat for some wind shifts until roughly

0Z/1Z this evening. 

There is ongoing potential for impacts to slant-range vis with

multiple fires continuing to burn in western Colorado, bringing

smoke into the urban corridor of the Front Range. Hi-res guidance

continues to keep the highest smoke concentrations to the north 

of all TAF sites through this evening, so any impacts would likely

be for any approaches from the north. For tomorrow morning,

guidance shows an increase in vertically integrated smoke over the

northeast plains but keeps the highest surface smoke 

concentrations generally over eastern Larimer and Weld Counties 

near sunrise. With surface smoke expected to stay to the north, 

went ahead and kept FU out of the TAF for now, but with the 

vertically integrated smoke increasing, there could be impacts to 

slant-range vis for the morning rush, especially for any 

approaches from the north and east. A swath of higher 

concentrations of smoke aloft will pass across the TAF sites 

through the late morning, but surface smoke should remain minimal. 

Westerly winds are expected for most of the day tomorrow across

the TAF sites, increasing between 18/19Z with some 20-25kt gusts

possible. Confidence is too low at this time to include any

mention in the TAF of gusty outflows from virga showers for the

afternoon, but this may need to be added to upcoming TAFs. 





