[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.1596","issue":"2025-08-07T01:13:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KLWX","awipsid":"AFDLWX"}]
=================================================



706

FXUS61 KLWX 070113

AFDLWX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC

913 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

High pressure will remain in place off the New England coastline 

through the end of the week, before becoming centered overhead this 

weekend. A cold front may approach from the northwest by the middle 

of next week.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH TONIGHT/...

Water vapor imagery shows an upper trough in place to our west 

over the Ohio Valley. This has moved slightly east through the

evening, bringing much drier air into our far western areas.  

Low-level moisture continues to stream northward in advance of 

this upper trough, resulting in a dense overcast shield along 

much of the Eastern Seaboard. This is starting to slowly erode

from west to east though as drier air pushes into the region.  

Steady rain remains primarily confined to far southern 

Maryland. The few lingering showers that were around this

evening have started to taper off at this point. While an

isolated shower can't be ruled out, dry conditions are expected

for most of the night. If breaks in the cloud cover are able to

develop, patchy fog may be possible later tonight, especially 

in sheltered river valleys west of the Blue Ridge. Low 

temperatures tonight will be in the 60s for most, with upper 50s

in the mountains.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT/...

High pressure will remain anchored off the New England coastline 

through Friday. Persistent onshore flow to the south of the high 

will maintain below normal temperatures across the area, with highs 

in the upper 70s to low 80s and lows in the upper 50s and 60s both 

days. Weak upper troughing will linger aloft tomorrow, before 

progressing offshore on Friday. A mix of clouds and sun is expected 

tomorrow, with potentially more clouds than sun by peak heating. 

More in the way of sunshine is expected on Friday as the upper 

trough departs to the east. Conditions should remain dry for the 

vast majority of the area on both Thursday and Friday, but an 

isolated popup shower can't be ruled out tomorrow afternoon, 

especially across the Central Shenandoah Valley or Central 

Virginia.



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

High pressure over Northeast US/Quebec will remain in control 

through the weekend. In turn, rain chances have decreased through 

the weekend as this high has trended slightly stronger and further 

south. Any measurable rainfall is likely confined to southern VA, 

with ensemble worst case scenarios bringing measurable rainfall 

into parts of central VA/southern MD over the weekend.  



Southeast US ridging will build through the early to middle portions 

of next week off shore bringing a return flow to the region. Moisture 

will be slow to return, though some guidance has spotty terrain 

driven convection Monday and Tuesday. Better chances for more 

widespread rain Wednesday or Thursday as trough approaches from 

Midwest.



Saturday will be a tick or two below normal with continued easterly 

flow. Temps moderate through the middle of next week as flow turns 

more southerly. In turn, moisture will return through mid week with 

a notable increase in humidity. Heat indices could approach 100F by 

the middle of next week.



&&



.AVIATION /01Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

MVFR ceilings may be possible for a time both tonight and 

tomorrow night, but otherwise VFR conditions are expected 

through Friday. For tonight, do think those odds are

decreasing, outside of CHO, so changed cloud levels to around 4

kft in the recent TAFs at all other sites. Winds will be remain

easterly through Friday, and could gust to around 15 knots 

tomorrow afternoon.



Mainly dry weather and light winds are expected through 

the weekend as high pressure to the northeast wins out. Some patchy 

fog or low clouds could develop each morning.



&&



.MARINE...

Persistent easterly winds are expected through Friday. Winds

have started to increase to SCA levels this evening. By later 

tonight, winds begin to increase over the wider waters, and 

then all waters by tomorrow afternoon. The easterly winds will 

drop back to sub-SCA level in nature by Friday.



Easterly flow continues over the weekend. Marginal advisory 

conditions may continue Saturday, especially along the middle bay, 

as low pressure move offshore. Pressure gradients weaken by Sunday, 

so lighter winds should prevail.



&&



.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...

Tidal anomalies will remain elevated over the next day or so due

to strengthening onshore flow. Minor coastal flooding is

possible at the end of the week with high astronomical tides due

to Full Moon on Saturday.



&&



.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

DC...None.

MD...Small Craft Advisory from noon to 6 PM EDT Thursday for MDZ008.

VA...None.

WV...None.

MARINE...Small Craft Advisory from noon to 6 PM EDT Thursday for ANZ530-

     535-536-538.

     Small Craft Advisory from 6 AM to 6 PM EDT Thursday for ANZ531-

     539.

     Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EDT Thursday for ANZ532>534-

     537-540>543.



&&



$$



SYNOPSIS...KJP

NEAR TERM...CJL/KJP

SHORT TERM...KJP

LONG TERM...CPB

AVIATION...CJL/KJP/CPB

MARINE...CJL/KJP/CPB

TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...LFR



